<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Need a Lot of Books! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> In the Library</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need a Lot of Books!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Identify specific information from instructions for a variety of activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify specific instructions for the library (e.g., 'You should be quiet in the library.').
            Can follow the thread of conversations in role-plays with multiple turns (e.g., 'How do I borrow a book?' 'Go to the library catalog.' 'What should I do next?' 'Take the card and write your name.').
            Can recognize essential details to complete library tasks (e.g., 'Write your name on the list.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of new vocabulary and pronunciation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing pictures of different places (a park, a store, a school, a library). Students listen to the teacher say the names of the places and point to the correct picture. The teacher then focuses on the 'library' picture and asks, "What do you do here?" (Listen and respond with gestures or simple words). The teacher holds up a book and asks, "What is this?" to introduce "book."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models 8-10 core vocabulary words related to the library: 'library', 'book', 'librarian', 'shelf', 'read', 'borrow', 'return', 'quiet', 'catalog', 'find'. The teacher clearly pronounces each word, showing flashcards or realia where possible. The teacher also models short sentences using modals like "You should be quiet in the library" and "Can I borrow this book?" For pronunciation, the teacher emphasizes the intonation for yes/no questions with modals (e.g., 'Can I take out this book?') and identifies short and long vowel sounds in words like /ɪ/ in 'pick' (from "pick a book") and /aɪ/ in 'find'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary and basic rules.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the library a place for loud talking?" (Expected answer: No.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you eat food in the library?" (Expected answer: No, you should be careful and quiet.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does a librarian do?" (Expected answer: Helps you find books.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__/__</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "In the Library".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays an audio recording of a short dialogue (approx. 1-1.5 minutes) between a student (e.g., Mia) and a librarian at the school library. The dialogue includes Mia asking how to borrow a book and the librarian giving clear, sequential instructions using imperatives and modals. (e.g., "Welcome to the library, Mia! How can I help you?" "I need a lot of books. How do I borrow a book?" "First, go to the library catalog. You should search for the title or author there. Then, find the book on the shelf. Finally, bring it to me at the checkout desk. You should write your name on the card." "Can I take out this book today?")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Asking for and giving information about resources: 'Can I borrow this book?'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio recording once for gist, then a second time for specific information.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "Where are Mia and the librarian?" (Expected answer: In the library.) "What is Mia trying to do?" (Expected answer: Borrow books.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students listen and mark statements as True or False.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Mia wants to return books. (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">The librarian tells Mia to go to the catalog first. (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Mia needs to write her name on a card. (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__/__</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening, practicing target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Identifying specific instructions, following sequential steps. Grammar: Modals 'can/should', Imperatives. Vocabulary: library, book, catalog, author, title, section, librarian, shelf, checkout, research, read, borrow, return, search, find, select, look up, quiet, careful, helpful, available.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Fill):</b> The teacher distributes a handout with sentences from the dialogue or similar instructions, but with missing target vocabulary words or modals. Students listen to the audio recording again (or the teacher rereads sentences) and fill in the blanks.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"You _____ be quiet in the _____." (should, library)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"Go to the _____ _____." (library catalog)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"Can I _____ this _____?" (borrow, book)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Listen and Sequence):</b> The teacher provides a list of jumbled instructions from the librarian (e.g., "Bring it to the checkout desk," "Find the book on the shelf," "Go to the library catalog," "Write your name on the card"). Students listen to the audio again and number the instructions in the correct order. The teacher can then check answers orally, prompting students to say the instructions aloud.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Draw/Point):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a simple diagram of a library with different sections (e.g., 'fiction shelf', 'non-fiction shelf', 'catalog computer', 'checkout desk'). Student A listens as Student B gives a series of 2-3 instructions (e.g., "Go to the fiction shelf." "Find an interesting book." "Then, go to the checkout desk."). Student A listens and draws a path on the diagram or points to the locations in order. Then, they switch roles.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__/__</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15-18 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "In the Library".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students work in pairs to role-play a scenario in the library. One student acts as a "New Library User" who needs to borrow books ("I Need a Lot of Books!") and asks questions about how to do it. The other student acts as the "Librarian" and gives instructions. The "New Library User" must actively listen to the librarian's instructions (e.g., "Go to the catalog and search for an author," "Select a book from the non-fiction section," "Bring it to the checkout desk," "You should sign your name here") to successfully complete the "borrowing" task. The "Librarian" practices asking and giving information, making polite requests, and using imperatives and modals appropriately. The teacher circulates, listening to student interactions and providing support as needed, focusing on listening comprehension and correct use of library vocabulary and grammatical structures. Students switch roles after a few minutes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__/__</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(8-10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play, focusing on students' ability to follow and give instructions accurately.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher gives three new, sequential library-related instructions (e.g., "Find a book about animals on the non-fiction shelf.", "Take it to the checkout desk.", "Return to your seat quietly."). Students listen and perform the actions (or mime them) in the correct order. The teacher observes if they followed the instructions correctly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students demonstrate comprehension by accurately following teacher's oral instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use target vocabulary (e.g., 'book', 'shelf', 'checkout', 'librarian', 'quiet') intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use modals ('should') and imperatives correctly when giving instructions in role-play.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students collaborate effectively in pair activities.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new library words did you learn or hear today?" (List 2-3)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you follow simple instructions about how to borrow a book in the library?" (Yes / A little / Not yet)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__/__</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher asks students to reflect individually or in small groups:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the easiest part for you when listening to the librarian's instructions today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was difficult, and what can you do to improve your listening next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, especially concerning the listening activities and the introduction of new vocabulary and grammar? (e.g., the audio dialogue was clear, students enjoyed the role-play).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to better support student learning? (e.g., some students struggled with sequencing instructions; more visual aids for the library layout might be helpful).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the learning outcomes met? How will this lesson prepare students for Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension)? (Students now have a good base of library vocabulary and an understanding of common instructions, which will be helpful when reading a text about the library).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__/__</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(2-3 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>